---
const parts = [
  ["Root", "The base meaning", "act → actor, react, action"],
  ["Prefix", "Changes the meaning, at the start", "unhappy, preview, disappear"],
  ["Suffix", "Changes the form, at the end", "kindness, hopeful, careless"]
];

const prefixes = [
  ["un-", "not", "unhappy"],
  ["dis-", "opposite", "dislike"],
  ["re-", "again", "rewrite"],
  ["pre-", "before", "preview"],
  ["mis-", "wrongly", "misunderstand"],
  ["in- / im- / ir- / il-", "not", "incorrect, impossible"]
];

const suffixes = [
  ["-ness", "a state or quality", "kindness"],
  ["-ful", "full of", "careful"],
  ["-less", "without", "hopeless"],
  ["-able / -ible", "can be", "readable, possible"],
  ["-er / -or", "a person who", "teacher, actor"]
];

const forms = [
  ["decide", "decision", "decide", "decisive", "decisively"],
  ["beauty", "beauty", "beautify", "beautiful", "beautifully"],
  ["create", "creation", "create", "creative", "creatively"]
];
---

<div class="topic-sheet">
  <h1>Word Families - Roots, prefixes and suffixes</h1>
  <p>A root is the main part of a word. A prefix or a suffix changes its meaning or its type.</p>
  <section class="summary-block summary-sand">
    <h2>The three parts</h2>
    <table class="punct-table summary-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th class="th-feature" scope="col">Part</th>
          <th class="th-rule" scope="col">Job</th>
          <th class="th-example" scope="col">Example</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          parts.map(([part, job, example]) => (
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">{part}</th>
              <td>{job}</td>
              <td>{example}</td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>
  <div class="summary-pair">
    <section class="summary-block summary-blue">
      <h2>Prefixes</h2>
      <table class="punct-table summary-table">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th class="th-feature" scope="col">Prefix</th>
            <th class="th-rule" scope="col">Meaning</th>
            <th class="th-example" scope="col">Example</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {
            prefixes.map(([prefix, meaning, example]) => (
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">{prefix}</th>
                <td>{meaning}</td>
                <td>{example}</td>
              </tr>
            ))
          }
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </section>
    <section class="summary-block summary-mint">
      <h2>Suffixes</h2>
      <table class="punct-table summary-table">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th class="th-feature" scope="col">Suffix</th>
            <th class="th-rule" scope="col">Meaning</th>
            <th class="th-example" scope="col">Example</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {
            suffixes.map(([suffix, meaning, example]) => (
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">{suffix}</th>
                <td>{meaning}</td>
                <td>{example}</td>
              </tr>
            ))
          }
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </section>
  </div>
  <section class="summary-block summary-lilac">
    <h2>Word forms</h2>
    <p>The same idea can be a noun, a verb, an adjective or an adverb. The sentence tells you which form it needs.</p>
    <table class="punct-table summary-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th class="th-feature" scope="col">Root</th>
          <th class="th-rule" scope="col">Noun</th>
          <th class="th-example" scope="col">Verb</th>
          <th class="th-trap" scope="col">Adjective</th>
          <th class="th-function" scope="col">Adverb</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          forms.map(([root, noun, verb, adjective, adverb]) => (
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">{root}</th>
              <td>{noun}</td>
              <td>{verb}</td>
              <td>{adjective}</td>
              <td>{adverb}</td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>“Choose the correct form.” He spoke very <strong>politely</strong>. “Which word is related to <em>construct</em>?” <strong>Construction</strong>, <strong>reconstruct</strong> and <strong>destructive</strong>.</p>
  </section>
</div>
